{"name":"Pattens Blue","hex":"#d3e5ef","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pattens","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of patten"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pattens"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Patten (shoe)","blocks":[{"text":"Pattens, also known by other names, are protective overshoes that were worn in Europe from the Middle Ages until the early 20th century. In appearance, they sometimes resembled contemporary clogs or sandals. Pattens were worn outdoors over a normal shoe, had a wooden or later wood and metal sole, and were held in place by leather or cloth bands. Pattens functioned to elevate the foot above the ground, to protect leather shoes from water, snow or mud. Women continued to wear pattens in muddy conditions until the 19th or even early 20th century."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Jan_van_Eyck_004.jpg/330px-Jan_van_Eyck_004.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patten_(shoe)","wikidata":"Q372381"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Walraversijde83.jpg/500px-Walraversijde83.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Walraversijde83.jpg/1280px-Walraversijde83.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Walraversijde83.jpg/500px-Walraversijde83.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hinged sole","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWalraversijde83.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Pattens.jpg/500px-Pattens.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Pattens.jpg/500px-Pattens.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Raised on iron rings","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APattens.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3e/1773_patten.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3e/1773_patten.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"A maid wearing circle-type pattens: Piety in Pattens or Timbertoe on Tiptoe, England 1773","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A1773_patten.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Femme_turque_avec_servante_%281742-1743%29_-_Jean-%C3%89tienne_Liotard_%28MAH%2C_Geneva%29.jpg/500px-Femme_turque_avec_servante_%281742-1743%29_-_Jean-%C3%89tienne_Liotard_%28MAH%2C_Geneva%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Femme_turque_avec_servante_%281742-1743%29_-_Jean-%C3%89tienne_Liotard_%28MAH%2C_Geneva%29.jpg/1280px-Femme_turque_avec_servante_%281742-1743%29_-_Jean-%C3%89tienne_Liotard_%28MAH%2C_Geneva%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Femme_turque_avec_servante_%281742-1743%29_-_Jean-%C3%89tienne_Liotard_%28MAH%2C_Geneva%29.jpg/500px-Femme_turque_avec_servante_%281742-1743%29_-_Jean-%C3%89tienne_Liotard_%28MAH%2C_Geneva%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tall pattens worn by two 18th-century Turkish women, pastel by Jean-Étienne Liotard, who visited Turkey in 1738","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFemme_turque_avec_servante_(1742-1743)_-_Jean-%C3%89tienne_Liotard_(MAH%2C_Geneva).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Walraversijde49.jpg/120px-Walraversijde49.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Walraversijde49.jpg/250px-Walraversijde49.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Walraversijde49.jpg/120px-Walraversijde49.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWalraversijde49.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Renaud_de_montauban_banquet_detail_poulaines.